      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To compare results of stenting in patients with and without prior coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In 21 patients after CABG stents were implanted both in venous grafts and native vessels (secondary revascularization) and in 25 patients without history of CABG native vessels were stented (primary revascularization)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Primary revascularization: there was 1 periprocedural non Q wave myocardial infarction (4%), angina disappeared in 88% and recurred in 1 year in 24% of patients; one year restenosis rate - 18.2%. Secondary revascularization: 1 periprocedural non Q wave (4.8%) and 1 Q wave (4.8%) myocardial infarction. Angina disappeared after stenting in 90.5% and recurred in 1 year in 19% of patients; one year restenosis rate - 17.6%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Both primary and secondary revascularizations were highly effective. However secondary stenting was associated with more frequent acute complications."
        }
      ]},
